// para.crdt — `LwwRegister`, a last-write-wins register: one value, overwritten, converging on the
// later write. The grow-only lattices beside it can only ever gain information, so none of them can
// say "the title is now *this*". A register can, at the price its name states: when two replicas
// write at once, one write is lost — and the whole design question is making that loss the *same*
// loss on every replica, which is what the causal stamp plus its tie-break buy.
// expect: stdout "unset=true empty=<lww unset>"
// expect: stdout "later_wins=final final"
// expect: stdout "concurrent=2 2 commutes=true"
// expect: stdout "value=[\"a\", \"b\"] shown=<lww [\"a\", \"b\"]>"
// expect: exit 0
use para.{crdt}

empty = crdt.lww()
echo "unset=${empty.get() == none} empty=${empty}"

// B wrote knowing A's write (it wrote to the state A's write produced), so B's write is causally
// later and wins from either direction.
a = crdt.lww().set("A", "draft")
b = a.set("B", "final")
echo "later_wins=${a.merge(b).get() ?? "-"} ${b.merge(a).get() ?? "-"}"

// Concurrent writes: both stamped from the same state, so neither saw the other and the logical
// times are equal. The replica id breaks the tie — the same way on both replicas, which is the
// difference between an arbitrary winner and a permanent disagreement.
x = crdt.lww().set("A", 1)
y = crdt.lww().set("B", 2)
echo "concurrent=${x.merge(y).get() ?? 0} ${y.merge(x).get() ?? 0} commutes=${x.merge(y) == y.merge(x)}"

// The value is whatever the program holds, not only a string: numbers, lists and maps ride along.
list = crdt.lww().set("A", ["a", "b"])
echo "value=${list.get() ?? []} shown=${list}"
